Not a terminal.

The want was never the grid — it was text that opens: file:line, url,
hash. tmux already hands over the word under the pointer, so owning the
grid buys none of it. mouse_word, copy_cursor_word, mouse_hyperlink,
copy-pipe; run-shell expands #{...}.

Measured on next-3.8: word-separators splits on / : . — the word under
ed/2-motion/04-fwd.go:12 comes back as `motion`. Set it to " " and the
whole address comes back. That one line is the enabler; without it the
extent has to be recomputed from mouse_x against the pattern.

The blocker is not the terminal. ed reads os.Args[1] and nothing else,
so `e +12 foo.go` opens a file named +12 — and a search hit has carried
that form all along, so the jump has never worked. Four lines.

Deferred, named:
  exec      the sibling verb — text that names an action, run it.
  relative  a path is relative to the cwd that printed it, not the cwd
            now. pane_current_path is right until a cd. acme solves it
            by owning the window — the price of not writing one.
  ed        third front end, text in a buffer. only if it earns it.

What a terminal would still buy is an editable output window. That is a
different want, and capture-pane -p | ed is the cheap half of it.
